<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo Reform Association</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo Reform Association was a humanitarian campaign founded in 1904 by the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-d-morel">E. D. Morel</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-casement">Roger Casement</a> to expose abuses in King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>. Using mass meetings, photographs and press campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, it helped force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> to annex the Congo in 1908 and was dissolved in 1913.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the mid-1890s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-d-morel">Edmund Dene Morel</a>, a shipping clerk for Elder Dempster in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, noticed that steady exports of firearms and cartridges to the Congo were matched by vast imports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and concluded that no real trade was taking place and that the goods were extracted by force. He resigned in 1901 and founded the West African Mail in 1903, drawing on missionaries, travellers and former agents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> and its concession companies. The Aborigine Protection Society under Henry Fox Bourne and Sir Charles Dilke MP had already denounced the regime.</p><p>A parliamentary debate in 1903 led the Foreign Office to send <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-casement">Roger Casement</a>, its consul at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boma">Boma</a>, into the interior. From June 1903 he recorded the testimony of victims and saw the mutilations himself. His report of 1904 confirmed the scale of the atrocities, though officials softened it and removed the names of witnesses and perpetrators. Disillusioned, Casement turned to Morel, and the two founded the association.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Morel led the campaign as a non-partisan and Christian cause and won the support of church leaders, businessmen, peers and MPs; Casement, as an official, stayed out of direct involvement. Regional branches spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and lectures used the magic lantern to show photographs of the atrocities. The missionaries John and Alice Harris gave some six hundred public talks in two years. The historian Felix Lösing has argued that racist dynamics, rather than evangelical philanthropy or humanitarian feeling, were the main reason for its success, since activists warned that the atrocities threatened imperial rule across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>An American Congo Reform Association, led by Baptist missionaries and the academic Robert E. Park and backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/booker-t-washington">Booker T. Washington</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a>, ran a parallel campaign. Leopold answered with a covert Press Bureau, which the Kowalsky Scandal of December 1906 exposed. On 23 January 1908 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> jointly demanded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> annex the territory, which it did later that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Reform after annexation was slow and gradual. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised the Belgian Congo in 1913, the association publicly disbanded on 16 June 1913, Morel declaring that the native of the Congo was once more a free man, although he and the movement knew this was not so.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congo Reform Association” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…whose photographs of mutilated people were shown at meetings across Britain and America, publicised the atrocities; the British consul Roger Casement’s report of 1904 gave them official weight; and Edmund Dene Morel’s <b>Congo Reform Association</b>, founded in 1904, built one of the first mass international human rights movements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
